{"authored" [{"question" "q-21536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+8.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(1)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(1)<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/state.json" "file_sha256" "b171229f63f2bc948afae3f1e5f84b115b2783e80bc4c5ea285748cf8ab15c71" "basis" 1229 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that $x=1$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+8,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+2x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=12{x}^{2}-12x+2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1) & =12(1)^{2}-12(1)+2 \\\\  & =2>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is positive, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/state.json" "file_sha256" "b171229f63f2bc948afae3f1e5f84b115b2783e80bc4c5ea285748cf8ab15c71" "basis" 1229 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033145/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "62bff426ce2a3